  "washed": [],
  "read": "The number fell to 21 (Extreme fear) from 27 at Tuesday's close, mostly because junk bonds lagged Treasuries (−2.3% over 10 days) and the fear curve rose to 1.08, but the tape said the opposite by the afternoon. Bad news stopped working: a hot core PCE (3.3% a year), new tariff plans and Nvidia down as much as 9% took SPY to −3.2% by 9:45, and it closed −0.3% (+1.9% from the open). Good and mixed news got bought for the first time this week: Dell filled its whole −9% gap by 2:05 p.m., Deere rose +4.5% on an earnings miss, Workday closed above Tuesday's close. Chips still closed red (NVDA −3.9%, MU −4.1%): sellers finished in the rest of the market first.",
  "positioned": "Still hedged: VIX closed 28.3 (+7%) even after the rebound, and the fear curve at 1.08 means traders kept paying up for near-term protection into a four-day weekend with a half-day Friday. Money rotated into equal-weight and cyclicals (RSP +0.8%, materials +2.1%, industrials, financials and small caps up) and out of tech (XLK −0.8%, SMH −1.0%). Few are long chips.",
  "surprise": "A crowd still braced (VIX 28, fear curve above 1.00) would be caught if Friday's thin session extends today's strength: SPY holding above 712.14 (Tuesday's low) with chips joining, NVDA reclaiming 150.50 and MU 609.80. The other side: tariff rates or a weekend headline that sends SPY back under 703.81 (today's opening-range high) would show it was holiday short-covering."
